    "seo": {
      "title": "Pahlavi Dynasty: State Building and Royal Power | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s Pahlavi period, from Reza Shah’s centralized state to oil nationalization, the White Revolution, and the monarchy’s collapse."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "state-and-monarchy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A stronger state and a stronger crown",
        "content": "The Pahlavi period lasted from 1925 to 1979. Reza Shah ruled until 1941, when the Allies forced his abdication; his son Mohammad Reza Shah succeeded him. Reza Khan’s earlier rise through the coup of 1921 connects the dynasty’s foundation to a broader reconstruction of central authority. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between state building and constitutional government is essential. Iran already possessed a constitutional framework and a Majles, or parliament. Under Reza Shah, ministries acquired written procedures, permanent offices, and more systematic records. Yet historian Alireza Sheikholeslami argues that accountability to parliament remained largely formal while administration served the monarch. Administrative modernization therefore did not necessarily produce restraints on royal power. [s1]\n\nCentralization also changed relations between Tehran and provincial society. The government reduced autonomous jurisdictions associated with tribal authorities and religious scholars, while expanding secular education and the bureaucracy. These developments increased the state’s reach into everyday life and displaced established sources of local authority. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "development-and-distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Development and its uneven consequences",
        "content": "Economic policy was not constant across the dynasty. Reza Shah initially operated within inherited commercial arrangements, but the Great Depression and disruption of export markets encouraged foreign-exchange controls and a state monopoly over foreign trade. The government became a central organizer of industrial development. The Trans-Iranian Railway, built between 1927 and 1938, illustrated this approach: a major national infrastructure project financed principally through additional taxes on tea and sugar. [s2]\n\nUnder Mohammad Reza Shah, the reform program known as the White Revolution combined social and economic intervention with the suppression of opposition. Land redistribution had begun before the program acquired that name. Its design distinguished between sharecroppers eligible to acquire redistributed land and exempted properties, including mechanized farms and certain plantations and orchards. Such distinctions matter: an announced national reform did not mean identical benefits for every rural household. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "oil-and-opposition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Oil, intervention, and the monarchy’s end",
        "content": "Oil nationalization brought the monarchy into a struggle involving Prime Minister Mohammad Mosaddegh, parliament, and foreign governments. Mosaddegh took office in 1951 with a mandate to implement nationalization of the British-owned Anglo-Iranian Oil Company. Historian Ervand Abrahamian’s examination of declassified documents places significant American involvement in Iranian politics before the eventual coup, rather than treating intervention as beginning suddenly in August 1953. [s3]\n\nThe coup overthrew Mosaddegh and strengthened the shah. Its later importance lay partly in how it connected royal power with foreign intervention in opponents’ understanding of the monarchy. Nevertheless, opposition to the shah was not a single movement: liberal nationalists, secular leftists, and different Islamic currents eventually participated in the revolutionary coalition. Their agreement on removing the monarch concealed substantial disagreements about what should replace him. [s3][s4]\n\nThe monarchy collapsed in February 1979. The subsequent struggle among revolutionary groups shows why the dynasty’s end and the consolidation of the Islamic Republic should be understood as connected but distinct historical processes. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
