    "description": "The Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828 confronted Qajar Iran with the expanding Russian Empire in the Caucasus. Military defeat and the settlements at Gulistan and Turkmenchay transformed Iran’s northwestern frontier and exposed the limits of both military reform and reliance on European allies. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Russo-Persian wars", "Qajar Iran", "Caucasus", "Abbas Mirza", "Gulistan", "Turkmenchay"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T13:48:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T13:48:25Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Russo-Persian Wars: Iran and the Caucasus | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand the wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, Abbas Mirza’s reforms, European diplomacy, and the changing Caucasian frontier."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "frontier-conflict",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A struggle over the Caucasus",
        "content": "Here, the Russo-Persian Wars refers primarily to the conflicts of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, rather than every earlier encounter between Russia and Iran. Their central setting was the Caucasus, the region between the Black and Caspian seas. The Russian Empire sought to consolidate its expansion while the Qajar monarchy defended its claims and authority. [s1][s2]\n\nRussian moves to annex Georgia preceded the first war. The capture of Ganja in January 1804 sharpened the confrontation. Fath-Ali Shah, Iran’s ruler, regarded the advance as a direct challenge and sent forces under his son Abbas Mirza, the crown prince and governor of Iranian Azerbaijan. The conflict involved competing claims over regional khanates—territories governed by khans—as well as confrontations between imperial armies. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "alliances-and-first-war",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why European alliances did not provide security",
        "content": "The first war was shaped by the Napoleonic conflicts in Europe. France promised assistance to Iran through the Treaty of Finkenstein in 1807, but Napoleon’s subsequent agreement with Russia at Tilsit undermined that commitment. Britain’s willingness to support Iran likewise changed with its relations with Russia. When Napoleon invaded Russia in 1812, Britain and Russia again became allies. [s1]\n\nThe lesson is more specific than a general claim that diplomacy failed: Iran’s prospective partners subordinated their Iranian commitments to wider strategic priorities. British mediation helped produce the Treaty of Gulistan in 1813, which ended the first war. [s1]\n\nDiplomatic activity continued afterward. Mirza Abu’l-Hasan Khan Ilchi, an Iranian envoy who had sought British help, traveled to St Petersburg in 1815 to pursue the return of occupied territories. That mission achieved no such restoration. His career shows how battlefield losses generated prolonged diplomatic efforts rather than a single, definitive moment of resignation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "second-war-and-reform",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Renewed war and the limits of reform",
        "content": "The Gulistan settlement did not resolve the confrontation permanently. In the renewed war of 1826–1828, Abbas Mirza suffered a major defeat near Ganja in September 1826. Russian forces subsequently penetrated Iranian Azerbaijan and occupied Tabriz in 1827. The settlement at Turkmenchay in 1828 brought further territorial loss and consolidated the separation of the territories beyond the Aras from Qajar rule. [s2]\n\nAbbas Mirza’s response to Russian military superiority included training troops along European lines. Tabriz also became a point of entry for foreign instructors, books, and educational initiatives; the prince sent young men to study in England. Reform emerged within the confrontation, rather than only after it. [s2]\n\nThese efforts did not reverse the military outcome. Their historical significance lies in the attempt to change the resources and organization available to the monarchy. The wars therefore belong simultaneously to the history of territorial contraction and to the early history of institutional reform in modern Iran. Reading them together avoids treating defeat as evidence that no adaptation occurred. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
